    <string name="tour_lost">The control for this step is not on this screen. Open the screen it belongs to, or leave the tour.</string>
    <string name="tour_list_title">Guided tours</string>
    <string name="tour_list_intro">Each one points at the real button on the real screen and waits for you to press it. Start any of them whenever you like — nothing here runs only once.</string>
    <string name="tour_list_start">Start</string>
    <string name="tour_list_done">seen</string>
    <string name="tour_first_steps_title">Your first colony</string>
    <string name="tour_first_steps_summary">From an empty account to a recorded inspection.</string>
    <string name="tour_first_steps_step1">An apiary is a place where your colonies stand — a garden, an orchard, a spot on a hill. Start with one.</string>
    <string name="tour_first_steps_step2">Give it the name you would use out loud. You will be reading it in the rain.</string>
    <string name="tour_first_steps_step3">Save it. Everything else hangs off an apiary.</string>
    <string name="tour_first_steps_step4">Open it. The colonies live inside an apiary.</string>
    <string name="tour_first_steps_step5">Add a colony. You do not need a printed QR code for this — the sticker is a shortcut to a colony that already exists, not a requirement for making one.</string>
    <string name="tour_offline_title">It works without a signal</string>
    <string name="tour_offline_summary">The one thing you cannot find out by looking, and the one that matters at the apiary.</string>
    <string name="tour_offline_step1">Record it here and it is written to the phone first, then sent. With no signal it waits, marked “Waiting to upload” in the list, and goes as soon as there is a connection. Nothing is lost if you walk out of range in the middle of a visit — which is the whole reason this screen works the way it does.</string>
    <string name="tour_qr_codes_title">QR stickers, and what they are for</string>
    <string name="tour_qr_codes_summary">A sheet of stickers, printed once, and a colony is one scan away for the rest of its life.</string>
    <string name="tour_qr_codes_step1">The QR batches live up here, in the apiary list’s toolbar. A batch is a sheet of stickers; the codes have to exist before they can mean a colony.</string>
    <string name="tour_qr_codes_step2">Make one. Take more stickers than you have colonies — a spare sheet in the shed costs nothing, and a sticker nobody has scanned yet belongs to no colony. You print the sheet from the website; this is where the codes come from.</string>
    <string name="tour_qr_codes_step3">And this is what they are for. The Scan tab opens the camera straight away — hold it at a sticker and that colony opens. No list, no searching, one hand free. A sticker nobody has scanned yet is offered to the next colony you make.</string>
    <string name="tour_hornets_title">A trap, and what you do with it daily</string>
    <string name="tour_hornets_summary">Register a named trap, then log what it caught. One number a day is the whole job.</string>
    <string name="tour_hornets_step1">The hornet tracker has its own tab. It is about Vespa velutina, the Asian hornet — not the European hornet, which is protected and does not belong in a trap.</string>
    <string name="tour_hornets_step2">A trap here is a physical trap of yours, in one place, with a name. Register it once.</string>
    <string name="tour_hornets_step3">A name you can tell apart in the dark — “behind the shed”, not “trap 2”. The position matters more than the name: the map is only as useful as the coordinates on it.</string>
    <string name="tour_hornets_step4">Register it. You get an eight-character code with it — that is how you reach this trap again from another device, without an account.</string>
    <string name="tour_year_title">The beekeeping year, shifted to your region</string>
    <string name="tour_year_summary">What happens when, and why the dates are not the same on a hill as in a valley.</string>
    <string name="tour_year_step1">The beekeeping year lives up here. What happens when — and the dates are not the same on a hill as in a valley, which is why they are shifted to your region.</string>
    <string name="tour_year_step2">And the dates move. A season that came three weeks early is not an error in the calendar, it is this year — so the calendar asks, here, and shifts the regional dates once enough beekeepers in one place have said the same thing.</string>
    <string name="tour_treatments_title">Treatments you will not have to remember</string>
    <string name="tour_treatments_summary">Plan one with a due date, tick it off when it is done, and the record writes itself.</string>
    <string name="tour_treatments_step1">Treatments belong to the apiary, not to one colony, because that is how varroa treatment is actually done. They are in the apiary’s toolbar.</string>
    <string name="tour_treatments_step2">Plan a treatment: what, and by when. It belongs to the whole apiary, because that is how varroa treatment is actually done — not one colony at a time.</string>
    <string name="tour_sharing_title">Letting somebody else see the apiary</string>
    <string name="tour_sharing_summary">An invitation by email, and what the other person may then do.</string>
    <string name="tour_sharing_step1">An apiary can be shared, from its toolbar. Whoever you let in sees it and records inspections in it; they cannot delete it, and you can take the access back at any time.</string>
    <string name="tour_sharing_step2">Invite them. They see this apiary and record inspections in it; they cannot delete it, and you can take the access back here at any time.</string>
    <string name="tour_custom_fields_title">Writing down what this app did not think of</string>
    <string name="tour_custom_fields_summary">Your own field on every inspection, in the shape you need it.</string>
    <string name="tour_custom_fields_step1">Nobody can guess what you record. Your own fields are here — mead batch, feed given, the queen’s temper.</string>
    <string name="tour_custom_fields_step2">Nobody can guess what you record. Mead batch, feed given, the queen’s temper — make the field yourself.</string>
    <string name="tour_moves_title">Moving colonies, and why it is worth recording</string>
    <string name="tour_moves_summary">Move several colonies to another apiary at once, and keep where they have been.</string>
    <string name="tour_moves_step1">Colonies travel — to the rape, to the heather, to a hive stand that stopped being the right one. Move them here rather than editing each colony in turn.</string>
    <string name="tour_moves_step2">And the map of moves, in the apiary list’s toolbar, draws the lines afterwards: where a colony stood and when is kept, so a year later you can still tell whether that heather trip was worth it.</string>
</resources>
